WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.260
You know, usually when we talk about a medical diagnosis, there's this expectation of like

00:04.260 --> 00:07.580
absolute precision. It feels almost like engineering.

00:07.800 --> 00:09.680
Oh, totally. It's very cut and dry.

00:09.800 --> 00:14.640
Right. You fall off your bike, your arm hurts, you go to the hospital and they take an x-ray

00:14.640 --> 00:18.400
and it shows that that jagged white line on the screen.

00:18.460 --> 00:21.900
Yeah. The doctor just points to it, says, you know, there it is. That's the break.

00:22.240 --> 00:27.440
Exactly. It's undeniable. But then when we step out of that individual exam room and

00:27.440 --> 00:33.360
into the world of, well, public health and epidemiology and how we as an entire society

00:33.360 --> 00:34.900
measure risk.

00:35.400 --> 00:36.760
Suddenly that x-ray machine is broken.

00:37.020 --> 00:41.760
It is completely broken. We are looking at a diagnostic landscape that is quite honestly

00:41.760 --> 00:44.520
just murky and it really plays tricks on our minds.

00:44.680 --> 00:46.880
It does. It fundamentally alters how we see things.

00:47.000 --> 00:52.060
So welcome to the deep dive. Today we are setting up a really mind bending premise that is going

00:52.060 --> 00:59.520
to challenge how you probably perceive medical statistics, public policy, and honestly, the news

00:59.520 --> 01:01.100
you read every single day.

01:01.220 --> 01:02.080
Yeah, it's a huge topic.

01:02.320 --> 01:09.040
It really is. So imagine you hear a widely cited statistic on like a news broadcast. The anchor

01:09.040 --> 01:15.760
looks into the camera very seriously and says, this new medical intervention, this vaccine,

01:16.080 --> 01:18.760
saved a thousand lives for every one person it injured.

01:18.880 --> 01:20.020
Right. The classic ratio.

01:20.020 --> 01:23.920
Yeah. And you naturally assume, as anyone would, that we are talking about a thousand

01:23.920 --> 01:25.540
and one completely different people.

01:25.660 --> 01:27.860
Of course. That's how our brains work.

01:27.920 --> 01:34.000
Right. You picture a thousand smiling, healthy people over here in this saved bucket and then

01:34.000 --> 01:36.860
one very unfortunate person over there in the injured bucket.

01:36.960 --> 01:38.520
The loser of the lottery, so to speak.

01:38.560 --> 01:43.800
Exactly. But what if the person who was saved and the person who was injured are the exact

01:43.800 --> 01:44.540
same person?

01:44.540 --> 01:50.300
I mean, it's a completely counterintuitive thought. It violates how we naturally, almost

01:50.300 --> 01:54.340
instinctively categorize winners and losers in any kind of public policy.

01:54.600 --> 01:57.140
We want clean lines. We want, you know, clear groups.

01:57.340 --> 02:02.740
We do. We crave that simplicity. But the math and more importantly, the fundamental logic of

02:02.740 --> 02:07.400
how we determine cause and effect tells us that this clean division we imagine in our heads

02:07.400 --> 02:09.040
is often a complete illusion.

02:09.040 --> 02:14.600
And breaking down that illusion is exactly our mission today. We are unpacking a set of

02:14.600 --> 02:21.320
fascinating, deeply interconnected concepts that deconstruct how we frankly, fundamentally

02:21.320 --> 02:23.580
misunderstand risk and statistics.

02:23.900 --> 02:26.040
And ultimately trust in the modern world.

02:26.180 --> 02:30.820
Right. We're going to look at something called the false partition in medical data and how both

02:30.820 --> 02:36.280
left-wing and right-wing political arguments completely misuse and misinterpret these statistics.

02:36.280 --> 02:39.060
It really is a bipartisan error. Both sides do it.

02:39.160 --> 02:43.820
Totally. And from there, we're going to look at the broader sociological fallout, like why

02:43.820 --> 02:48.740
modern society is fracturing into these completely separate tribes that can't even agree on basic

02:48.740 --> 02:49.400
facts anymore.

02:49.820 --> 02:51.680
The trust apocalypse, is the source calling.

02:51.800 --> 02:55.920
Yes, the trust apocalypse. But just a quick heads up as we get into this, you know, we are going to

02:55.920 --> 03:01.740
be looking at some highly charged topics today, specifically COVID-19 vaccine data and the

03:01.740 --> 03:03.520
collapse of institutional trust.

03:03.520 --> 03:07.180
Because that's the lens the researchers use to study these phenomena.

03:07.540 --> 03:14.160
Exactly. But our goal here is absolutely not to take a political or medical side. We are just using

03:14.160 --> 03:18.940
this as a map to look at the underlying math and the behavioral psychology of how human beings

03:18.940 --> 03:19.900
calculate risk.

03:20.080 --> 03:21.760
Yeah, we're totally impartial guides here.

03:21.920 --> 03:26.380
We're just here to map out the architecture of the arguments so you can understand the profound

03:26.380 --> 03:28.920
ideas driving so much of the debate we see today.

03:29.020 --> 03:30.280
Which is so needed right now.

03:30.280 --> 03:36.320
So let's unpack this from the very beginning. The core logical flaw that drives so much public

03:36.320 --> 03:42.180
outrage seems to come down to how we interpret a single rhetorical device, the ratio.

03:42.360 --> 03:47.360
Yes, the ratio is really the battlefield of public discourse. Defenders of a policy will use this

03:47.360 --> 03:54.340
ratio of, you know, lives saved versus injuries caused to prove that safety concerns are statistically

03:54.340 --> 03:54.980
negligible.

03:54.980 --> 03:59.900
They point to the numerator and say, look at the millions saved compared to the handful harmed.

04:00.020 --> 04:05.220
Exactly. They say the intervention is obviously good. Meanwhile, critics of the policy look at the exact

04:05.220 --> 04:11.140
same ratio. But they isolate the denominator, the injury counts, and use those raw numbers to prove the

04:11.140 --> 04:13.140
intervention is unacceptably dangerous.

04:13.140 --> 04:17.460
So they're looking at the exact same numbers, but taking completely different lessons.

04:17.660 --> 04:24.360
Right. But what's fascinating here is that both sides, despite arguing fiercely with one another,

04:24.500 --> 04:28.120
are actually accepting the exact same mathematical fallacy.

04:28.500 --> 04:28.960
Which is?

04:29.080 --> 04:30.960
They are both accepting the false partition.

04:31.080 --> 04:38.980
The idea that the population divides cleanly into two entirely separate, mutually exclusive sets of people.

04:38.980 --> 04:44.740
Yes, exactly. The beneficiaries on the left side of the room and the victims on the right side of the room.

04:44.880 --> 04:46.340
Like it's a school dance or something.

04:46.480 --> 04:52.160
Yeah, exactly. The false partition treats that statistical ratio as if it were a moral census.

04:52.700 --> 04:59.600
If you have a highly favorable ratio, say 10,000 to one, you imagine a population made up of mostly

04:59.600 --> 05:02.660
helped people and a tiny fraction of harmed people.

05:02.740 --> 05:07.720
But that gloss is entirely false, even if the underlying ratio itself is mathematically accurate.

05:07.720 --> 05:13.600
Right. A serious adverse event like a rare medical injury and a prevented death do not have to belong

05:13.600 --> 05:19.400
to disjoint groups of people. They can be potential components of the exact same person's medical outcome.

05:19.680 --> 05:25.700
This gets into the fundamental problem of causal inference, right? Because for any given person,

05:26.140 --> 05:29.720
you, me, anyone listening, we only get to observe one timeline.

05:30.540 --> 05:31.740
We only get one reality.

05:31.860 --> 05:36.220
Right. We see the person who got the intervention, but they're counterfactual.

05:36.220 --> 05:39.860
What would have happened to them in the alternate reality where they didn't get it is completely

05:39.860 --> 05:43.920
unobservable. We don't have a parallel universe window to look through.

05:44.220 --> 05:47.560
And that unobservability is the crux of the entire issue.

05:48.400 --> 05:52.100
Let's ground this in a real documented medical example to make it clear.

05:52.240 --> 05:52.920
OK, let's do it.

05:53.020 --> 05:55.700
Let's say a young person receives an mRNA vaccine.

05:55.700 --> 06:02.040
And as the 2024 report from the National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine confirmed

06:02.040 --> 06:07.800
can happen in rare cases, this person develops a vaccine attributable case of myocarditis.

06:07.920 --> 06:09.720
Which is an inflammation of the heart muscle.

06:09.840 --> 06:13.720
Yes. Now that is a genuine harm. It was causally produced by the intervention.

06:14.200 --> 06:16.700
We can document it clinically. We can see it on a scan.

06:16.760 --> 06:19.100
So they are without a doubt vaccine injured.

06:19.100 --> 06:25.380
Yes. But that fact alone, the existence of the injury, does not tell us they're counterfactual.

06:25.720 --> 06:25.960
Oh.

06:26.140 --> 06:31.560
What if that exact same person, given their specific biology and exposure risk, would have

06:31.560 --> 06:36.660
contracted a severe, perhaps fatal case of the disease had they remained unvaccinated?

06:36.720 --> 06:38.240
Oh, wow. I see what you're saying.

06:38.300 --> 06:43.120
Right. If that were true, their counterfactual outcome would have been significantly worse

06:43.120 --> 06:44.740
than the myocarditis they experienced.

06:44.740 --> 06:49.940
So being accurately classified as vaccine injured does not inherently mean that the person's

06:49.940 --> 06:52.260
net outcome was worse than the alternative.

06:53.060 --> 06:58.540
Exactly. But because the prevented death is a counterfactual, it's invisible. We can only

06:58.540 --> 07:03.140
estimate it on a population level. We can never, ever prove it for that specific individual

07:03.140 --> 07:04.380
standing in front of us.

07:04.500 --> 07:07.060
Wait, listening to explain this, it feels like it's like a seatbelt.

07:07.080 --> 07:08.400
A seatbelt. Okay, run with that.

07:08.420 --> 07:14.580
Imagine you get into a really severe high-speed car crash. The force of the impact causes the

07:14.580 --> 07:18.700
shoulder strop of the seatbelt to lock up, and it actually snaps your collarbone.

07:18.820 --> 07:19.420
Ouch, yeah.

07:19.760 --> 07:24.820
Right. You are genuinely injured by the seatbelt. You can go to the hospital, take an x-ray,

07:25.320 --> 07:28.820
point to the broken bone, and say the seatbelt absolutely caused this.

07:28.900 --> 07:30.060
It's a documented harm.

07:30.240 --> 07:35.920
Exactly. But the counterfactual is that without the seatbelt, given the physics of the crash,

07:35.920 --> 07:39.840
you would have gone face first through the windshield and died on the highway.

07:40.160 --> 07:40.260
Right.

07:40.260 --> 07:45.980
So you are both the victim of the seatbelt and its beneficiary simultaneously. The injury

07:45.980 --> 07:52.740
is a visible, documented fact, but the death it prevented is an invisible, alternative reality.

07:53.040 --> 07:57.940
That is a phenomenal analogy, and it perfectly highlights why the false perdition is so misleading.

07:58.440 --> 08:03.440
The only way the false perdition actually works, the only way the saved and the injured are guaranteed

08:03.440 --> 08:08.320
to be completely separate groups of people, is if we assume a statistical concept called

08:08.320 --> 08:09.340
monotonicity.

08:09.440 --> 08:13.540
Okay, let's break that down, because monotonicity sounds like something from an advanced calculus

08:13.540 --> 08:16.280
class. What does that actually mean in plain English?

08:16.380 --> 08:21.020
Yeah, it sounds intense. In mathematics, a monotonic function is one that only ever moves

08:21.020 --> 08:23.740
in one direction. It never reverses course.

08:23.900 --> 08:26.100
Okay, so it just goes up, or it just goes down?

08:26.100 --> 08:33.920
Exactly. Translated to public health, monotonicity is the assumption that an intervention never,

08:33.920 --> 08:40.280
ever makes anyone worse off in any dimension whatsoever. It assumes the intervention only

08:40.280 --> 08:43.500
pushes health outcomes in a positive direction.

08:43.500 --> 08:45.980
But that's obviously not true for medicine, right?

08:45.980 --> 08:51.640
No, of course not. If monotonicity were true, then yes, the people who suffered an adverse

08:51.640 --> 08:56.780
event must be a totally separate group from the people who were helped, because the intervention

08:56.780 --> 08:59.200
is incapable of doing both to the same person.

08:59.380 --> 09:05.620
But the data explicitly rejects monotonicity. I mean, we just talked about the NOSM 2024 report

09:05.620 --> 09:10.600
confirming a causal link to myocarditis. Medical interventions have side effects. That's

09:10.600 --> 09:11.660
just a known reality.

09:11.660 --> 09:16.660
Precisely. Medical interventions are complex biological tools. They're not magic wands. So

09:16.660 --> 09:19.940
monotonicity does not hold. People do experience genuine harm.

09:20.080 --> 09:20.300
Right.

09:20.500 --> 09:25.420
But the critical realization is that without monotonicity, we cannot mathematically prove

09:25.420 --> 09:30.240
that the people who experienced the rare harm weren't also the exact same people who were

09:30.240 --> 09:31.560
saved from a much greater harm.

09:31.620 --> 09:35.360
So the math simply does not allow us to draw that clean dividing line.

09:35.440 --> 09:36.040
It doesn't.

09:36.060 --> 09:41.340
Which brings up something called freshet inequalities, which, again, sounds incredibly intimidating.

09:41.340 --> 09:43.260
I know. Another huge math term.

09:43.640 --> 09:47.680
But I want to make sure we really grasp this, because it seems like the key to understanding

09:47.680 --> 09:52.840
how blind we actually are when we read the news. Can we walk through a concrete example

09:52.840 --> 09:54.940
of how this plays out in a population?

09:55.400 --> 10:00.880
Let's do it. Think of freshet inequalities not as complex equations, but simply as a way

10:00.880 --> 10:02.940
of defining the boundaries of our ignorance.

10:03.260 --> 10:05.080
I like that. Boundaries of ignorance.

10:05.220 --> 10:10.200
They tell us the maximum and minimum possible overlap between two events when we only know the

10:10.200 --> 10:10.760
total numbers.

10:10.900 --> 10:11.800
Okay. Lay it up for me.

10:12.600 --> 10:18.600
Imagine a town of 100,000 people. A new virus sweeps through, and the public health officials

10:18.600 --> 10:23.960
roll out a treatment. After everything is said and done, the epidemiologists look at the data

10:23.960 --> 10:26.460
and determine two top-line statistics.

10:26.600 --> 10:27.580
Okay. Tracking with you.

10:27.580 --> 10:33.340
First, the treatment prevented severe disease in 10% of the town, so 10,000 people were saved from

10:33.340 --> 10:40.040
the ICU. Second, the treatment caused a mild adverse reaction in 1% of the town, so 1,000 people

10:40.040 --> 10:41.020
had a bad reaction.

10:41.280 --> 10:44.780
Okay, so I'm reading the newspaper in this town, and the headline says,

10:45.440 --> 10:53.300
Treatment saves 10,000, harms 1,000. I immediately picture 11,000 distinct people involved in this

10:53.300 --> 10:53.560
drama.

10:53.560 --> 10:56.320
Exactly. That's the false partition at work right there in your head.

10:56.420 --> 10:56.640
Ah.

10:56.960 --> 11:01.900
But what do the freshet inequalities tell us? They tell us to look at the overlap.

11:02.080 --> 11:02.340
Okay.

11:02.960 --> 11:10.420
Scenario A is the maximum overlap. Is it mathematically possible that all 1,000 of the people who had

11:10.420 --> 11:14.820
the bad reaction were also part of the 10,000 people whose lives were saved?

11:14.900 --> 11:16.220
I mean, logically, yes.

11:16.220 --> 11:23.620
Yes. In that scenario, 100% of the injured are actually net beneficiaries who avoided the ICU

11:23.620 --> 11:25.560
at the cost of a mild reaction.

11:25.780 --> 11:26.560
Wow. Okay.

11:26.940 --> 11:32.200
And then scenario B is the minimum overlap. Is it mathematically possible that the 1,000 people

11:32.200 --> 11:35.400
who had the reaction were completely separate from the 10,000 who were saved?

11:35.480 --> 11:36.520
Yeah, it's possible too.

11:36.640 --> 11:41.400
Yes. In that scenario, those 1,000 people took a risk, got no benefit, and suffered a harm.

11:41.540 --> 11:44.460
So they really are just victims in that specific scenario.

11:44.460 --> 11:49.300
Right. And the freshet inequalities simply state that the true reality could be anywhere

11:49.300 --> 11:55.000
between those two extremes. The marginal counts. The top-line numbers in your newspaper headline

11:55.000 --> 11:58.660
simply cannot tell us where the truth lies within that range.

11:58.780 --> 12:02.760
That is wild to think about. They cannot tell us the true distribution of human welfare.

12:03.000 --> 12:03.920
No, they can't.

12:03.960 --> 12:08.580
When you read a headline that says 1,000 people saved for every one person injured,

12:08.580 --> 12:15.280
you have to actively train your brain to realize that it is comparing two pools of events,

12:15.380 --> 12:20.320
not necessarily separating human beings into two distinct, non-overlapping camps.

12:20.440 --> 12:22.520
Exactly. It's events, not people.

12:23.060 --> 12:26.960
And because we have this massive blind spot when it comes to individuals,

12:27.200 --> 12:31.080
because we can't see the counterfactual and the math allows for this hidden overlap,

12:31.860 --> 12:35.440
scientists and policymakers naturally turn to population-level statistics.

12:35.440 --> 12:36.660
Right. They zoom out.

12:36.780 --> 12:38.980
If we can't figure it out perfectly for one person,

12:39.180 --> 12:42.820
let's look at a million people and see if the overall trend is positive.

12:43.180 --> 12:48.000
But zooming out creates a whole new set of problems, doesn't it? It leads to massive

12:48.000 --> 12:53.660
category errors where completely different types of data are just mashed together in public discourse.

12:53.780 --> 12:54.400
Oh, absolutely.

12:54.720 --> 12:58.360
It's almost like we're speaking two different scientific languages without realizing it.

12:58.360 --> 13:05.140
It's exactly like that. The public debate constantly conflates vaccine injury across two entirely

13:05.140 --> 13:07.400
different scales of scientific description.

13:07.620 --> 13:09.020
Okay. What are the two scales?

13:09.380 --> 13:14.400
On one hand, you have clinical causal inference. This is the domain of the doctor in the exam room.

13:14.640 --> 13:18.460
It asks a highly specific, individualized question.

13:19.580 --> 13:23.920
Did this specific medical intervention harm this specific person sitting in front of me?

13:24.400 --> 13:25.300
Very one-on-one.

13:25.300 --> 13:29.840
Right. On the other hand, you have population epidemiology. This is the domain of public

13:29.840 --> 13:34.180
health officials looking at spreadsheets of millions of data points. It asks a totally

13:34.180 --> 13:40.520
different question. Does this policy reduce severe outcomes overall across an entire society?

13:41.100 --> 13:42.840
So they are fundamentally different tasks.

13:43.240 --> 13:46.640
Completely different scientific disciplines. They use different methodologies,

13:46.760 --> 13:50.640
different standards of evidence, and have totally different goals. But in the public discourse,

13:50.640 --> 13:54.820
we use the exact same phrase, vaccine injury, to talk about both.

13:55.180 --> 13:59.940
And that linguistic confusion leads to what you could call mismatched numerators.

14:00.160 --> 14:02.160
Oh, the mismatched numerators are everywhere.

14:02.380 --> 14:08.980
I see this all the time online. Someone will post a link to a massive, highly complex modeling study,

14:09.180 --> 14:15.480
like the one that estimated 14.4 to 19.8 million deaths were averted globally in the first year of the

14:15.480 --> 14:17.220
COVID-19 vaccination program.

14:17.220 --> 14:20.020
A huge, robust statistical model.

14:20.080 --> 14:26.320
Right. And then someone in the comments will try to debunk that by pulling up a raw, unverified list of injury reports

14:26.320 --> 14:30.960
from a database like VAEAR, the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System.

14:31.020 --> 14:32.300
It happens constantly.

14:32.460 --> 14:36.860
They just divide the massive global model by the raw, passive surveillance counts and say,

14:36.920 --> 14:38.540
look, the math proves you're wrong.

14:38.600 --> 14:42.360
Which is mathematically absurd. It is a profound category error.

14:42.360 --> 14:42.980
How so?

14:43.320 --> 14:48.080
It's like taking a multinational corporation's estimated annual global revenue,

14:48.860 --> 14:53.760
dividing it by the amount of cash sitting in the register of a single local mom and pop store

14:53.760 --> 14:58.780
on a random Tuesday, and pretending the resulting ratio tells you something meaningful about the

14:58.780 --> 14:59.500
global economy.

14:59.560 --> 15:01.040
That is such a good way to put it.

15:01.320 --> 15:09.180
You are taking a transmission model fit to excess mortality data across 185 countries over a specific

15:09.180 --> 15:15.680
12-month window, and you are pairing it against a totally open-ended, self-reported, cumulative passive

15:15.680 --> 15:21.620
surveillance total. Those numbers were never, ever designed to speak to one another.

15:22.120 --> 15:27.760
It's totally apples and oranges. When people scream, is it safe online, I always feel like they are asking

15:27.760 --> 15:31.060
a completely different question than a regulator or a scientist is asking.

15:31.180 --> 15:31.860
They absolutely are.

15:31.920 --> 15:36.880
The public wants a binary yes or no, but a scientist doesn't even define the word safety that way, do they?

15:36.880 --> 15:41.340
No, they don't. And this is a massive source of the disconnect. When a scientist or medical

15:41.340 --> 15:46.140
regulator evaluates safety, they aren't looking for a binary switch. They are actually running

15:46.140 --> 15:49.240
through a very deliberate four-stage inferential process.

15:49.480 --> 15:52.040
Okay, walk me through the four stages. What's step one?

15:52.460 --> 15:58.160
Question one is pharmacovigilance. This is pure signal detection. Is there a signal? Does this

15:58.160 --> 16:04.520
intervention produce an elevation in some outcome, literally any outcome, above what the normal

16:04.520 --> 16:06.340
background rate in the population would predict?

16:06.440 --> 16:07.280
So it's just a tripwire.

16:07.520 --> 16:12.220
It's exactly a tripwire. Question two is causal inference. Okay, the tripwire went off. Given

16:12.220 --> 16:18.100
that there is a signal, how large is the actual effect? Who is most at risk? And what is the

16:18.100 --> 16:23.340
margin of uncertainty? We need to prove the intervention actually caused the tripwire rather

16:23.340 --> 16:24.680
than it just being a coincidence.

16:25.040 --> 16:27.460
Right, because correlation is not causation.

16:27.680 --> 16:33.980
Exactly. Then question three is risk analysis. Now we take those proven estimated harms and we weigh them

16:33.980 --> 16:38.320
against the harms of the disease itself or the harms of doing nothing at all. This is where we balance

16:38.320 --> 16:38.880
the scales.

16:39.460 --> 16:45.440
Okay, so we've found a signal, proven it's real, and weighted against the alternative. What's the last step?

16:45.640 --> 16:51.580
Finally, question four is decision theory. Given everything we've learned in the first three steps,

16:52.220 --> 16:58.640
what policy action is actually preferable for the society? Should we authorize it? Should we restrict

16:58.640 --> 16:59.860
it to certain age groups?

17:00.300 --> 17:06.100
But in the public sphere, we just smash all four of those distinct, nuanced scientific questions

17:06.100 --> 17:11.060
into one giant glowing red button labeled, is it safe? Yes or no?

17:11.200 --> 17:17.860
Yes. And smashing those questions together causes two mirror image errors that perfectly define our

17:17.860 --> 17:19.560
political polarization on this issue.

17:19.700 --> 17:20.860
Okay, what are the two errors?

17:21.220 --> 17:25.060
Well, one side of the debate treats any detected harm at the pharmacovigilance stage.

17:25.060 --> 17:29.040
The mere tripping of the wire is automatically decisive against the entire policy.

17:29.300 --> 17:31.880
They act like the risk analysis stage doesn't even exist.

17:32.080 --> 17:36.440
Right. They say if it caused one harm, it is fundamentally unsafe and must be stopped.

17:36.540 --> 17:37.260
And on the other side?

17:37.360 --> 17:42.500
The other side makes the exact opposite error. They treat a favorable policy decision at stage four

17:42.500 --> 17:48.120
as evidence that no harm exists at all. They act as though the decision to authorize the intervention

17:48.120 --> 17:52.780
could only be correct if the pharmacovigilance question had come back completely negative.

17:52.780 --> 17:58.600
So they say if the CDC says it's good policy, then anyone claiming they were injured must be lying.

17:58.840 --> 18:04.900
Exactly. Both sides are mistaking a single stage of the scientific process for the whole thing.

18:05.040 --> 18:09.920
Okay, let's drill down into the data sources people use to fight these battles because you mentioned

18:09.920 --> 18:14.720
VA errors earlier, and I think that's one of the most misunderstood databases in the entire world.

18:14.800 --> 18:15.460
Oh, without a doubt.

18:15.460 --> 18:22.120
People treat a VA errors report, a government compensation claim, and a formally adjudicated

18:22.120 --> 18:27.760
scientific link as if they are completely interchangeable proofs of injury. But they

18:27.760 --> 18:29.800
are astronomically different things, right?

18:29.880 --> 18:35.960
They are completely different species of information. Let's start with VA errors, the passive reporting

18:35.960 --> 18:41.080
system. Its entire purpose is that first stage we talked about, pharmacovigilance.

18:41.140 --> 18:41.900
A tripwire.

18:41.900 --> 18:47.000
Right. It is an early warning system designed for hypothesis generation and signal detection.

18:47.480 --> 18:51.480
Because it's just a tripwire, the system is designed to be as sensitive as possible,

18:51.680 --> 18:54.800
which means literally anyone can file a report.

18:54.880 --> 18:56.420
Right, like literally anyone.

18:56.560 --> 19:01.160
A doctor can file a report. A patient can file a report. A parent can file a report.

19:01.640 --> 19:06.180
If you receive a vaccine on Monday and you get struck by lightning on Tuesday,

19:06.580 --> 19:08.980
you can theoretically report that to VARS.

19:09.600 --> 19:10.080
Wow.

19:10.080 --> 19:15.200
The system is designed to catch temporal proximity, things happening close together in time,

19:15.580 --> 19:17.640
not to establish medical causation.

19:17.860 --> 19:22.320
So a high number of VAS reports doesn't mean a high number of proven injuries. It just means

19:22.320 --> 19:25.440
the tripwire is highly sensitive, like it's supposed to be.

19:25.500 --> 19:31.000
Exactly. Then, moving up the ladder of complexity, you have compensation programs,

19:31.300 --> 19:34.580
like the U.S. National Vaccine Injury Compensation Program.

19:34.740 --> 19:37.020
Which is where the government actually pays out money.

19:37.020 --> 19:41.540
Right. But these do not operate on strict scientific standards of absolute proof.

19:41.860 --> 19:45.200
They operate on a legal, administrative, or precautionary standard.

19:45.720 --> 19:50.220
They might pay out a financial claim, simply because an injury falls within a predefined time

19:50.220 --> 19:52.760
window on a predetermined table of possibilities.

19:53.080 --> 19:58.040
It's a much more permissive standard than, say, publishing a paper in a medical journal.

19:58.040 --> 19:59.620
Vastly more permissive.

20:00.500 --> 20:05.880
And finally, at the very top, you have formal causal adjudication, like the National Academy

20:05.880 --> 20:12.340
Standard. This requires rigorous epidemiological, clinical, and biological evidence to look at

20:12.340 --> 20:18.820
the data and say definitively, yes, this specific product biologically causes this specific outcome.

20:18.820 --> 20:24.800
So treating a raw surveillance count from a sensitive tripwire system like VA-ers as if

20:24.800 --> 20:30.600
it were a list of formally adjudicated, scientifically proven injuries is a profound error.

20:30.720 --> 20:34.020
It's a massive mistake, and it completely ignores the base rate problem.

20:34.060 --> 20:39.320
Oh, the base rate problem. Yes, this is crucial for understanding how epidemiology actually works.

20:39.560 --> 20:43.020
Because if you were vaccinating hundreds of millions of people over a short period of time,

20:43.440 --> 20:45.320
human biology doesn't just hit pause.

20:45.320 --> 20:47.300
No, the background noise is deafening.

20:47.420 --> 20:50.680
Imagine a massive city of a million people on any given Tuesday.

20:51.220 --> 20:54.200
In that city, just by the laws of statistics and human health,

20:54.620 --> 20:56.760
a certain number of people are going to have a heart attack today.

20:57.000 --> 20:57.700
Sadly, yes.

20:57.940 --> 20:59.760
A certain number are going to have a stroke.

21:00.460 --> 21:04.000
A certain number of pregnancies will unfortunately end in miscarriage.

21:04.680 --> 21:08.260
Now imagine you give everyone in that city a glass of water at 9 a.m.

21:08.940 --> 21:11.460
By 2 p.m., someone will have had a stroke.

21:12.100 --> 21:14.300
If you aren't careful, you might blame the water.

21:14.300 --> 21:17.560
That is the base rate problem, perfectly articulated.

21:18.220 --> 21:21.440
When you intervene on a population of hundreds of millions of people,

21:22.000 --> 21:26.960
thousands of adverse medical events are going to happen in the days and weeks following the intervention,

21:27.420 --> 21:32.460
entirely by coincidence, just because those things happen every single day in a population that large.

21:32.580 --> 21:34.540
Because biology is just messy.

21:34.760 --> 21:35.320
Exactly.

21:35.660 --> 21:41.720
You cannot treat a raw surveillance count as if the baseline expectation of human health is zero.

21:42.180 --> 21:43.400
The baseline is not zero.

21:43.400 --> 21:50.320
The baseline is the expected background incidence of those conditions in a comparable, unintervened population.

21:50.720 --> 21:53.140
Without comparing the raw reports to that background rate,

21:53.460 --> 21:57.200
the raw count is scientifically uninformative as an estimate of actual harm.

21:57.680 --> 21:58.580
It is just noise.

21:58.660 --> 21:59.200
It's noise.

21:59.420 --> 22:03.640
You know, listening to you explain this, it feels like building a fire break in a forest.

22:03.780 --> 22:04.520
A fire break.

22:04.560 --> 22:04.800
Okay.

22:04.800 --> 22:11.760
Imagine a massive, catastrophic wildfire is sweeping across a mountain range, heading straight for a town.

22:11.760 --> 22:18.860
The firefighters bring in bulldozers, and they aggressively cut down hundreds of healthy trees to create a gap,

22:19.080 --> 22:21.280
a fire break, to stop the blaze.

22:21.500 --> 22:22.840
To starve the fire of fuel.

22:22.920 --> 22:23.180
Right.

22:23.500 --> 22:27.560
If you just look at the fire break in isolation, it looks like environmental destruction.

22:27.780 --> 22:29.800
You had to cut down trees to save the forest.

22:29.800 --> 22:34.920
But someone standing right there, looking at the stumps, is just going to call you a tree killer.

22:35.260 --> 22:36.580
They missed the whole context.

22:36.740 --> 22:37.160
Exactly.

22:37.540 --> 22:45.240
They are naming the intervention by its small, localized cost, while totally ignoring the catastrophic destruction it just prevented.

22:45.720 --> 22:49.580
That analogy captures the essence of the category error perfectly.

22:49.580 --> 22:56.220
The local description, the trees were cut down or an adverse medical event occurred, is materially true.

22:56.820 --> 22:59.080
You aren't lying when you say the trees are gone.

22:59.160 --> 23:00.020
The harm is real.

23:00.360 --> 23:05.820
But the characterization of the intervention's broader social function is taxonomically perverse.

23:06.480 --> 23:11.040
You are defining the act of salvation by the inevitable friction it took to achieve it.

23:11.260 --> 23:15.440
But this raises a really interesting and, frankly, very difficult tension.

23:15.440 --> 23:21.460
When those government compensation programs actually pay out millions of dollars to people who claim they were harmed,

23:21.800 --> 23:26.780
when the government physically writes a check, the public sees that and says,

23:26.980 --> 23:29.240
They see it as a smoking gun.

23:29.340 --> 23:29.600
Yes.

23:29.960 --> 23:31.040
They say, see?

23:31.720 --> 23:33.660
The government is literally paying them off.

23:33.900 --> 23:35.460
That proves the vaccine is dangerous.

23:35.640 --> 23:36.340
And they know it.

23:36.400 --> 23:37.400
But they're pushing it anyway.

23:37.980 --> 23:41.320
Doesn't paying people off naturally breed massive distrust?

23:41.800 --> 23:44.760
It's completely understandable why the public reacts that way.

23:44.760 --> 23:45.740
I mean, it looks like guilt.

23:46.340 --> 23:50.820
But from a structural policy perspective, the argument is actually the exact opposite.

23:51.060 --> 23:51.540
How so?

23:51.780 --> 23:55.180
A compensation program is not a guilty concession that undermines confidence.

23:55.600 --> 23:59.360
It is actually an expression of profound institutional confidence.

23:59.480 --> 23:59.940
Really?

24:01.220 --> 24:02.580
Think about the underlying logic.

24:03.800 --> 24:09.100
The state is arguing that the medical intervention is so overwhelmingly beneficial to the society as a whole

24:09.100 --> 24:11.840
that it warrants collective mass deployment.

24:11.840 --> 24:18.960
But because there is an unpreventable, extremely rare, adverse tale to that policy,

24:19.360 --> 24:21.040
because biology is messy, like you said,

24:21.420 --> 24:29.560
it is fundamentally unethical to let the severe costs of that policy fall arbitrarily on the few individuals who draw the short straw.

24:29.900 --> 24:31.140
Oh, so it's about fairness.

24:31.140 --> 24:31.740
Exactly.

24:33.020 --> 24:40.440
Compensation socializes the rare, adverse tale of a policy in exactly the same way the vaccine socializes protection against the infection.

24:40.940 --> 24:41.760
It spreads the burden.

24:42.240 --> 24:44.480
It doesn't transform a good policy into a bad one.

24:44.980 --> 24:49.120
It is simply the ethical management of residual risk in a complex society.

24:49.120 --> 24:53.320
Wow. So if the population level math is overwhelmingly positive,

24:53.600 --> 24:58.020
and if compensating the injured is actually a sign of ethical, confident policymaking,

24:58.560 --> 25:01.260
why is the injury narrative so incredibly dominant?

25:01.400 --> 25:02.320
Because of human nature.

25:02.480 --> 25:04.480
Right. Like, why is it all we seem to talk about?

25:04.740 --> 25:09.440
If the math is overwhelmingly positive, but we are mathematically blind to the people being saved,

25:09.580 --> 25:12.340
that explains why the stories of injury are so loud.

25:12.560 --> 25:15.420
You can't put a microphone in front of a prevented hospitalization.

25:15.420 --> 25:21.180
Precisely. And this gets to the core of why human beings struggle so deeply with preventative medicine.

25:21.840 --> 25:25.160
It comes down to what the data leaves behind in the real world.

25:25.900 --> 25:27.700
What do you mean by what it leaves behind?

25:28.120 --> 25:31.060
Well, a prevented event has no phenomenology.

25:31.420 --> 25:31.900
Phenomenology.

25:31.980 --> 25:34.620
Right. It leaves no sensory trace in human experience.

25:35.060 --> 25:38.200
You do not experience the severe pneumonia you didn't catch.

25:38.340 --> 25:40.680
You don't notice the ventilator that was never required.

25:40.900 --> 25:41.720
It tells nothing happened.

25:42.120 --> 25:42.600
Exactly.

25:42.600 --> 25:48.340
You cannot wake up, point to a specific date on a calendar, and say with absolute certainty,

25:48.720 --> 25:50.800
that is the day the vaccine saved my life.

25:50.940 --> 25:51.060
Right.

25:51.520 --> 25:58.440
Prevention appears socially as a complete absence, and an absence does not spontaneously generate testimony.

25:59.140 --> 26:05.020
No one writes a passionate Facebook post about the Tuesday where absolutely nothing biologically notable happened to them.

26:05.120 --> 26:07.160
But an adverse event is the exact opposite.

26:07.280 --> 26:07.860
It's loud.

26:07.860 --> 26:15.320
It is vivid. It has a specific date. It has a medical diagnosis. It has a human face. It involves pain, fear, and disruption.

26:15.740 --> 26:21.240
It can be narrated as a deeply personal chronological story with a clear villain and a clear victim.

26:21.240 --> 26:27.680
So the public archive of stories, the collective narrative we all draw from when trying to understand the world,

26:28.040 --> 26:32.840
is not a neutral, randomized sample of the intervention's actual causal effects.

26:32.900 --> 26:33.380
Not at all.

26:33.700 --> 26:38.060
It is selectively populated only by the outcomes that are capable of becoming stories.

26:38.840 --> 26:42.020
The intervention's costs produce highly motivated witnesses.

26:42.020 --> 26:48.900
Its successes systematically remove the very events that would have produced witnesses to its necessity.

26:49.440 --> 26:50.980
That is such a heavy realization.

26:51.740 --> 26:56.820
The people who were saved have literally no reason to attribute anything to the intervention,

26:57.140 --> 26:59.760
because their lives just continued uninterrupted.

26:59.820 --> 27:02.540
They went to the grocery store, they picked up their kids, they lived.

27:02.760 --> 27:08.240
But the person who got sick immediately, and understandably, searches for an explanation.

27:08.240 --> 27:13.840
And this asymmetry and visibility is supercharged by the way human psychology is hardwired, right?

27:14.240 --> 27:16.440
We aren't rational calculating machines.

27:16.560 --> 27:18.520
No, we are very emotional creatures.

27:18.720 --> 27:23.640
The research by psychologists like Daniel Kahneman and Amos Traversky plays a huge role here.

27:23.920 --> 27:28.300
Yes. Their work on prospect theory is fundamental to understanding this dynamic.

27:29.200 --> 27:32.340
Human judgment does not weigh gains and losses equally.

27:32.520 --> 27:33.580
We focus on the negative.

27:33.580 --> 27:38.600
We naturally, evolutionarily, weight loss is far more heavily than commensurate gains.

27:39.260 --> 27:43.960
Losing $100 feels significantly worse than finding $100 feels good.

27:44.160 --> 27:47.680
So an outcome that is a loss, an adverse medical event,

27:47.840 --> 27:50.760
and is already the only side of the ledger that produces a vocal witness

27:50.760 --> 27:55.960
is doubly disadvantaged in public perception because our brains are wired to fixate on the loss.

27:56.060 --> 28:00.140
Exactly. And then you compound that with the identifiable victim effect.

28:00.140 --> 28:08.900
An individuated victim with a face, a name, and a heartbreaking story elicits a massive visceral emotional response.

28:09.340 --> 28:12.680
But a statistically described group facing a massive aggregate harm,

28:12.820 --> 28:18.180
like excess mortality in the over 65 cohort, elicits almost zero emotional response.

28:18.320 --> 28:21.560
Right. Our brains just aren't built to feel empathy for a spreadsheet.

28:21.780 --> 28:25.300
But there is another layer to this that goes beyond just psychology and narrative.

28:25.300 --> 28:28.480
It gets into actual, measurable biology.

28:28.880 --> 28:32.100
And this absolutely blew my mind when I was reading through the research.

28:32.360 --> 28:33.160
The nocebo effect.

28:33.480 --> 28:35.960
Yes. We've all heard of the placebo effect.

28:36.360 --> 28:40.100
Your brain believes you are taking medicine, so your body actually starts to heal.

28:40.480 --> 28:43.800
But there's a dark mirror to that called the nocebo effect.

28:43.940 --> 28:45.340
Which is incredibly powerful.

28:45.700 --> 28:51.560
This is where you experience genuine adverse physical symptoms simply because you expect to.

28:51.560 --> 28:57.240
What did the data actually show in the COVID-19 vaccine trials regarding this?

28:57.940 --> 29:03.200
This is one of the most critical and honestly least understood aspects of public health communication.

29:04.240 --> 29:10.920
In a massive systematic review of 12 randomized placebo-controlled COVID-19 vaccine trials,

29:11.340 --> 29:13.520
researchers looked closely at the control groups.

29:13.620 --> 29:15.360
The people who didn't get the real shot.

29:15.460 --> 29:20.460
Right. These are the people who received an injection of completely inert saline, essentially salt water.

29:20.500 --> 29:21.140
Just salt water.

29:21.140 --> 29:27.380
They found that 35.2% of those placebo recipients reported at least one systemic adverse event,

29:27.900 --> 29:32.420
like a severe headache, acute fatigue, or muscle pain, after the first dose.

29:32.540 --> 29:33.740
Wait, let me make sure I'm hearing you right.

29:33.820 --> 29:40.300
Over a third of the people who got injected with nothing but salt water experienced genuine systemic physical pain or fatigue.

29:40.460 --> 29:41.060
Over a third.

29:41.320 --> 29:44.960
Is the pain real or is it just in their heads? Are they making it up?

29:45.060 --> 29:47.920
It is not made up. They are not lying. The pain is real.

29:47.920 --> 29:56.700
The nocebo effect is a measurable biological phenomenon where negative expectations translate into actual neurochemical changes in the body,

29:57.040 --> 29:59.800
producing inflammation, pain signals, and fatigue.

29:59.800 --> 30:05.040
Your brain anticipates harm and it physically manifests the symptoms of that harm.

30:05.040 --> 30:20.240
Yes. The analysis of those trials estimated that roughly three quarters of the systemic adverse events reported by the actual vaccine recipients were likely attributable to this nocebo component rather than the pharmacological action of the vaccine itself.

30:20.240 --> 30:28.060
That is staggering. Three quarters. And what makes it so dangerous in our modern world is that these expectations of harm can be socially transmitted, right?

30:28.260 --> 30:28.660
Completely.

30:28.780 --> 30:34.560
It's not just the doctor warning you about side effects. It's you scrolling through social media while sitting in the waiting room.

30:34.560 --> 30:46.320
Yes. It is a highly efficient, socially mediated learning pathway. Hearing about someone else's severe side effects or watching a viral video of someone describing their symptoms

30:46.320 --> 30:54.040
can actually increase the biological likelihood that a subsequent recipient will experience and report those exact same symptoms themselves.

30:54.160 --> 30:57.860
So it feeds a continuous narrative expectations symptom cycle.

30:57.860 --> 31:04.700
Exactly. And again, this highlights the profound asymmetry of public health because there is no equivalent positive cycle.

31:05.260 --> 31:11.660
Hearing a viral story about someone who didn't get pneumonia doesn't biologically increase your own lung capacity.

31:12.020 --> 31:14.320
The nocebo effect only amplifies the negative.

31:14.520 --> 31:15.280
Only the negative.

31:15.460 --> 31:20.560
Okay. I have to jump in and push back here on behalf of the listener because this is a really, really delicate line to walk.

31:20.660 --> 31:21.040
Let's do it.

31:21.240 --> 31:25.360
We cannot just write off someone's genuine pain and suffering as a nocebo artifact.

31:25.360 --> 31:34.760
If a young man develops severe myocarditis or someone suffers a massive blood clot, they didn't just imagine it into existence because they read a scary tweet.

31:35.120 --> 31:40.080
We can't use psychology to gaslight people out of very real medical injuries.

31:40.440 --> 31:41.360
I agree emphatically.

31:42.140 --> 31:44.760
And it is crucial to be painstakingly clear on this point.

31:44.760 --> 31:57.280
This is not an attempt to wave a psychological magic wand and reclassify causally adjudicated pharmacologically proven harms like the mRNA myocarditis link as mere nocebo artifacts.

31:57.400 --> 31:57.660
Right.

31:57.820 --> 32:00.920
Those are real biological product-caused effects.

32:01.620 --> 32:05.060
The point is absolutely not to deny genuine injury.

32:05.280 --> 32:06.380
So what is the point?

32:06.380 --> 32:17.320
The point is that how we communicate about those rare, genuine injuries in a public forum can socially amplify additional expectation-driven symptoms in hundreds of thousands of other people.

32:17.540 --> 32:18.480
Ah, I see.

32:18.660 --> 32:25.800
Leaving the communication unmanaged, just letting the raw, terrifying stories dominate the algorithm isn't a neutral choice for a society.

32:26.200 --> 32:33.840
It's a choice to let the testimonial asymmetry operate completely unchecked, which actively degrades the overall health and well-being of the population.

32:33.840 --> 32:37.680
It's what sociologists call the prevention paradox in public health.

32:38.160 --> 32:47.080
The interventions that provide the most massive aggregate benefits to society as a whole often offer almost zero perceptible benefit to the individuals participating in them,

32:47.340 --> 32:53.800
which means public health messaging is permanently structurally vulnerable to vivid anecdotes of harm.

32:54.040 --> 32:54.260
Yes.

32:55.080 --> 33:02.240
Epidemiology is literally the only tool we have that makes prevented outcomes inferentially visible at all.

33:02.240 --> 33:05.200
It is the only way to see the people who were saved.

33:05.540 --> 33:09.240
But if the public loses confidence in the institutions that produce that epidemiology...

33:10.000 --> 33:17.440
If they stop trusting the people doing the math, then the visible, narratable, emotional side of the comparison wins by default.

33:17.700 --> 33:21.860
And that perfect storm of lost confidence is exactly where we find ourselves today.

33:21.860 --> 33:31.000
If the population-level math is overwhelmingly positive, but the public refuses to look at the epidemiological data entirely because of the asymmetry of visibility,

33:31.360 --> 33:35.440
we arrive at a much broader, much more dangerous societal crisis.

33:35.500 --> 33:36.680
Like structural crisis.

33:36.960 --> 33:39.980
This is where the math problem turns into a sociological meltdown.

33:40.380 --> 33:43.180
Let's look at what researchers are calling the trust apocalypse.

33:43.180 --> 33:51.220
The trust apocalypse is an umbrella term utilized by thinkers like Kieran McCammon and cognate scientist John Verveke to describe the sweeping,

33:51.440 --> 33:57.180
almost breathtaking popular disillusionment with the core sense-making institutions of modern society.

33:57.520 --> 34:00.980
Science, journalism, medicine, the courts.

34:01.320 --> 34:01.740
Exactly.

34:02.080 --> 34:04.060
And the data backing this up is stark.

34:04.640 --> 34:08.120
Gallup polling, which has tracked institutional confidence for decades,

34:08.120 --> 34:15.780
shows that average confidence across key U.S. institutions dropped from a high of around 48% in 1979

34:15.780 --> 34:20.520
to a record historic low of just 28% heading into 2025.

34:20.800 --> 34:22.340
Wow, 48 down to 28.

34:23.020 --> 34:28.100
But the researchers argue that just calling it a trust apocalypse and throwing our hands up is actually too coarse.

34:28.240 --> 34:28.960
Just too simplistic.

34:29.220 --> 34:32.000
It's a blunt instrument that hides what's really happening.

34:32.000 --> 34:40.160
It compresses what is actually three completely distinct, separable sociological processes into one scary buzzword.

34:40.340 --> 34:41.160
Right, let's break them down.

34:41.280 --> 34:44.420
Those three processes are decline, fragmentation, and migration.

34:44.740 --> 34:46.860
Let's really break those down, starting with decline.

34:47.180 --> 34:50.960
Decline is the most straightforward, and it's the one the media focuses on the most.

34:51.420 --> 34:58.560
It simply means that fewer people across the population as a whole express confidence in a given institution today than they did in previous decades.

34:58.760 --> 35:00.920
That's the top-line Gallup number dropping.

35:00.920 --> 35:03.880
Exactly, from 48% to 28%.

35:03.880 --> 35:06.100
It's a general erosion of faith.

35:06.960 --> 35:14.580
But decline alone doesn't tell the whole story, because it implies everyone is losing trust at the exact same rate, which just isn't true.

35:15.040 --> 35:16.560
Right, because then you have fragmentation.

35:16.860 --> 35:17.060
Yes.

35:17.580 --> 35:21.900
Fragmentation means the population no longer converges on which institutions deserve confidence.

35:22.740 --> 35:25.240
Trust is no longer a shared national consensus.

35:25.240 --> 35:31.180
It becomes deeply patterned by prior identity, most often political or cultural affiliation.

35:31.280 --> 35:32.440
So we split into camps.

35:33.040 --> 35:35.280
Look at the Gallup data regarding mass media.

35:36.220 --> 35:39.020
Confidence in the news hasn't just dropped overall.

35:39.380 --> 35:46.640
It is completely bifurcated along intense partisan lines, with groups moving rapidly toward opposite ends of the scale.

35:46.640 --> 35:55.280
It's not that people have stopped trusting entirely, they are still trusting, but they are only trusting mutually exclusive, highly polarized sets of authorities.

35:55.480 --> 35:55.760
Exactly.

35:56.000 --> 35:56.640
They trust their team.

35:56.860 --> 35:59.160
Which brings us to the third process, migration.

35:59.660 --> 36:02.320
And to me, this is the most fascinating part of the whole analysis.

36:02.320 --> 36:05.760
Because trust isn't just evaporating into thin air, it's energy.

36:05.860 --> 36:06.740
It has to go somewhere.

36:07.000 --> 36:08.080
It is relocating.

36:08.420 --> 36:17.160
People are migrating away from broad, low-commitment ecumenical institutions, like a national news network or a massive federal agency.

36:17.500 --> 36:22.380
And they are moving toward narrow, high-commitment, highly demanding communities.

36:23.020 --> 36:30.500
The sociological data points to a very specific and somewhat paradoxical religious trend to illustrate this migration.

36:30.660 --> 36:31.680
Oh, this is so interesting.

36:31.680 --> 36:39.720
Amidst a broader, decades-long narrative of general secularization and the rapid decline of mainstream, low-barrier religious affiliation,

36:40.200 --> 36:46.880
there is a measurable, distinct growth in some of the most doctrinally demanding and traditional institutions available.

36:47.160 --> 36:47.540
Like what?

36:47.920 --> 36:56.560
Specifically, sociologists are tracking a migration of conservative young men moving toward Eastern Orthodoxy or traditional Latin Mass Catholicism.

36:56.740 --> 36:57.480
Okay, wow.

36:57.480 --> 37:07.420
These are institutions that demand a tremendous amount from you, strict fasting rules, long and complex liturgies, highly distinct lifestyle practices that separate you from the secular world.

37:07.680 --> 37:08.780
Okay, but why those?

37:08.880 --> 37:16.120
If someone is just looking for community because they don't trust the CDC or CNN anymore, why not just join a local book club or a softball league?

37:16.160 --> 37:16.260
Right.

37:16.380 --> 37:17.240
Why the high demand?

37:17.240 --> 37:24.160
Why migrate toward institutions that demand you give up eating meat on Fridays and attend three-hour services in a dead language?

37:24.520 --> 37:27.980
Because of how human beings evolutionarily evaluate truth.

37:28.900 --> 37:37.420
There is a fascinating theory by anthropologist Joseph Henrich concerning what he calls credibility-enhancing displays, or CREDs.

37:37.500 --> 37:38.080
CREDs.

37:38.160 --> 37:39.380
Okay, tell me about CREDs.

37:39.380 --> 37:44.220
The theory posits that human beings, as cultural learners, are not indiscriminate imitators.

37:44.720 --> 37:48.120
We don't just believe whatever anyone tells us because talk is cheap.

37:48.680 --> 37:52.320
And evolutionarily, believing cheap talk gets you killed or exploited.

37:52.400 --> 37:52.940
Makes sense.

37:53.160 --> 37:58.100
We evolved to weight the truth of a belief by the visible costliness of the model's commitment to it.

37:58.640 --> 38:03.660
We only believe what someone preaches if they are willing to pay a visible, costly price for that belief.

38:03.660 --> 38:13.680
So, in an environment where trust is fragmenting and everyone is accusing everyone else of lying, we look for the highest possible price tag on a belief to ensure it's not a scam.

38:14.060 --> 38:14.460
Exactly.

38:14.980 --> 38:24.100
Institutions that require visible, costly displays of commitment, CREDs, transmit belief much more credibly than low-cost, easy-to-join institutions.

38:24.760 --> 38:33.020
A traditional religious community, with strict fasting rules and demanding physical rituals, provides constant, public, unfakeable legibility of commitment.

38:33.020 --> 38:34.260
It proves they mean it.

38:34.520 --> 38:44.100
A broad, low-commitment, secular institution, like a federal health agency or a national newspaper, structurally cannot replicate that kind of costly signaling.

38:44.760 --> 38:49.860
Therefore, in a low-trust environment, the high-commitment enclave wins the battle for belief.

38:50.360 --> 38:53.100
But wait, is the trust apocalypse actually a new phenomenon?

38:53.340 --> 38:54.300
I mean, think about history.

38:54.300 --> 39:01.420
Before the mid-20th century, before mass broadcast media and three dominant TV networks told everyone the exact same facts every night,

39:01.720 --> 39:08.680
didn't we always trust our tight-knit enclaves and high-commitment communities more than distant, faceless, bureaucratic institutions?

39:09.100 --> 39:10.540
That is a very astute question.

39:10.660 --> 39:14.300
Isn't tribalism just the historical baseline for human sociology?

39:14.300 --> 39:18.040
It directly addresses what researchers call the baseline problem.

39:19.080 --> 39:24.560
The data notes that confidence in U.S. institutions has been declining continuously since 1979.

39:25.400 --> 39:26.960
So what is actually new here?

39:27.160 --> 39:31.780
What makes it an apocalypse today, rather than just a return to the historical mean?

39:31.920 --> 39:32.920
Right. What changed?

39:33.220 --> 39:35.760
The underlying shift is the end of information scarcity.

39:35.760 --> 39:44.620
In the past, the mere fact that an institution had the resources to endorse a claim, like printing a CDC logo on a nationally distributed public health pamphlet,

39:44.940 --> 39:48.960
was a scarce, incredibly expensive, and therefore informative signal.

39:49.060 --> 39:52.180
It proved that a massive apparatus had vetted the information.

39:52.440 --> 39:52.660
Yes.

39:53.240 --> 40:00.160
Now, in the digital age, that same official logo is just one pixelated image drowning in an infinite ocean of competing claims,

40:00.900 --> 40:04.980
slickly produced independent podcasts, and highly committed online affinity groups.

40:04.980 --> 40:06.220
It's just a JPEG.

40:06.560 --> 40:07.060
Exactly.

40:07.740 --> 40:11.460
The marginal evidential value of institutional endorsement has plummeted to zero,

40:11.660 --> 40:16.220
regardless of whether the institution itself has actually become any less scientifically reliable.

40:16.580 --> 40:20.080
Which leads us directly into the core crisis of our modern age.

40:20.900 --> 40:26.380
If trust has migrated out of the center and into these high-commitment, disparate, fragmented enclaves,

40:26.840 --> 40:29.360
how do facts travel between them?

40:29.420 --> 40:30.640
That's the million-dollar question.

40:30.640 --> 40:34.680
How does a scientific discovery made in one tribe get accepted by another?

40:34.980 --> 40:38.240
And the terrifying answer seems to be, it doesn't.

40:38.700 --> 40:41.780
And this brings us to the concept of epistemic transport.

40:42.280 --> 40:48.920
Epistemic transport is defined as the capacity for a claim that is certified as reliable in one social location

40:48.920 --> 40:54.000
to remain usable as accepted evidence in another distant social location.

40:54.260 --> 40:56.200
Can you give me an example of how that should work?

40:56.200 --> 40:59.940
Think of how knowledge is supposed to flow in a functioning society.

41:00.660 --> 41:05.740
A laboratory finding informs a regulatory decision, which informs a mainstream news report,

41:06.120 --> 41:10.320
which ultimately informs an ordinary conversation between two neighbors over a fence.

41:10.480 --> 41:11.700
Right, the chain of information.

41:11.920 --> 41:16.100
For that system to work, the factual claim has to carry enough of its original warrant,

41:16.300 --> 41:19.060
its original authority, along every step of the journey,

41:19.060 --> 41:24.020
so that the neighbors don't have to literally re-derive the cellular biology from scratch themselves.

41:24.340 --> 41:26.180
It is like an electrical grid.

41:26.320 --> 41:28.000
Okay, I like your analogies.

41:28.200 --> 41:32.680
Think about a personal story, like a vivid emotional video of someone on TikTok

41:32.680 --> 41:35.820
talking about their severe vaccine injury.

41:36.500 --> 41:39.820
That video is like a standalone battery pack.

41:39.900 --> 41:44.360
You can hand it to anyone, anywhere, in any enclave, and it powers up immediately.

41:44.620 --> 41:45.440
Because it's a story.

41:45.440 --> 41:48.360
It doesn't require any external infrastructure to work.

41:48.460 --> 41:51.360
You just watch the video, you see the person's face, you hear their pain,

41:51.500 --> 41:54.920
and your human empathy immediately believes they experienced it.

41:54.980 --> 41:56.400
It transports effortlessly.

41:56.640 --> 42:03.500
But epidemiological data, like the statistical claim that a global vaccine rollout saved 14 million lives,

42:03.980 --> 42:09.980
is like a massive industrial appliance that requires a highly complex centralized power grid to function.

42:09.980 --> 42:16.840
It relies on multi-country transmission models, excess mortality data, international health agencies,

42:17.140 --> 42:18.300
and peer-reviewed journals.

42:18.640 --> 42:21.320
If you don't trust the grid, if you think the power company is corrupt,

42:21.680 --> 42:23.660
the appliance is completely useless.

42:23.920 --> 42:24.820
It's dead on arrival.

42:25.080 --> 42:30.020
That analogy perfectly captures why epistemic transport fails in a fragmented society.

42:30.680 --> 42:33.220
Personal narrative transports perfectly without trust.

42:33.620 --> 42:36.440
Statistical models cannot transport an inch without it.

42:36.440 --> 42:36.880
Wow.

42:37.180 --> 42:42.960
And researchers have identified a specific, highly destructive psychological mechanism for this failure

42:42.960 --> 42:44.940
called recursive suspicion.

42:45.140 --> 42:48.580
Okay, this is the really dark part of human psychology.

42:49.540 --> 42:51.820
Walk us through recursive suspicion.

42:52.360 --> 42:54.020
Ordinary skepticism is healthy.

42:54.360 --> 42:55.700
It is the foundation of science.

42:56.460 --> 43:01.380
Ordinary skepticism dedupts a specific, localized claim and asks for better evidence.

43:01.380 --> 43:06.120
I don't think that drug works as well as you say, show me the trial data.

43:06.260 --> 43:07.200
That's just being smart.

43:07.400 --> 43:07.560
Right.

43:07.840 --> 43:09.940
But recursive suspicion is something entirely different.

43:10.120 --> 43:11.840
It doubts the entire chain of arrival.

43:12.220 --> 43:13.540
It doesn't just doubt the claim.

43:13.760 --> 43:15.540
It doubts the researchers' incentives.

43:15.860 --> 43:17.760
It doubts the university's funding sources.

43:17.980 --> 43:19.000
Oh, I see where this is going.

43:19.160 --> 43:22.020
It doubts the journal editor's political selection criteria.

43:22.460 --> 43:26.320
It doubts the social media algorithm that curated the feed to show you the article.

43:26.620 --> 43:30.420
It even doubts the listener's own cognitive vulnerability to being manipulated.

43:30.420 --> 43:35.160
Because every single step in that chain of information is mediated by another institution,

43:35.960 --> 43:39.200
the suspicion recurses endlessly, like a hall of mirrors.

43:39.600 --> 43:43.280
It never terminates in a checkable, solid fact.

43:43.420 --> 43:43.800
Exactly.

43:43.940 --> 43:44.660
It just keeps going.

43:44.740 --> 43:47.980
But if I'm an institution, if I'm the CDC or a major university,

43:48.400 --> 43:51.100
and people are doubting me with this recursive suspicion,

43:51.840 --> 43:54.780
my natural instinct is to just show them more data.

43:55.040 --> 43:56.460
I want to turn up the volume.

43:56.540 --> 43:56.740
All right.

43:56.800 --> 43:57.820
You want to prove them wrong.

43:57.820 --> 44:00.440
I want to point to the spreadsheet and say, look at the numbers.

44:00.600 --> 44:01.720
The numbers prove I'm right.

44:02.020 --> 44:04.440
Why does that strategy fail so miserably?

44:04.940 --> 44:08.100
Because it completely misunderstands the nature of the suspicion.

44:08.800 --> 44:14.700
When suspicion is recursive, supplying additional evidence through the exact same distrusted channel

44:14.700 --> 44:16.360
doesn't resolve the doubt.

44:16.800 --> 44:18.000
It actually fuels it.

44:18.020 --> 44:18.840
It makes it worse.

44:18.840 --> 44:19.380
Yes.

44:20.220 --> 44:24.320
The audience reads the sheer volume and aggressive presentation of the new evidence

44:24.320 --> 44:29.380
as further definitive confirmation that the process is untrustworthy.

44:29.520 --> 44:33.460
They think, if the data is so obvious, why are you trying so hard to convince me?

44:33.500 --> 44:34.680
You must be hiding something.

44:34.960 --> 44:35.360
Exactly.

44:35.820 --> 44:38.000
It triggers a defensive amplification dynamic.

44:38.000 --> 44:41.900
This maps perfectly onto the work of psychologist Paul Slovic, right?

44:42.360 --> 44:44.720
His research on how humans perceive risk.

44:45.120 --> 44:50.600
Because the audience in these digital spaces is operating on what he calls risk as feelings.

44:51.200 --> 44:52.420
Yes, risk as feelings.

44:52.560 --> 44:57.580
They are processing the issue through affect, emotion, image, and narrative.

44:57.580 --> 45:04.260
But the institutions are stubbornly trying to communicate via risk as analysis, probabilities,

45:04.560 --> 45:06.980
standard deviations, and counterfactual models.

45:07.140 --> 45:08.640
They're speaking completely different languages.

45:09.020 --> 45:11.220
And when epistemic trans-court breaks down,

45:11.580 --> 45:16.900
the feelings-based mode completely ruthlessly displaces the analytic mode in public judgment.

45:17.060 --> 45:17.380
Exactly.

45:17.820 --> 45:20.400
A personal injury account arrives with a human face,

45:20.860 --> 45:24.580
a clear chronological narrative, and vivid, visceral emotion.

45:24.580 --> 45:27.980
It is tailor-made for our evolutionary risk as feelings hardware.

45:28.180 --> 45:29.020
It slots right in.

45:29.160 --> 45:31.560
But a huddled reduction in global mortality, however,

45:31.720 --> 45:35.900
is merely a mathematical difference between two counterfactual population distributions.

45:36.720 --> 45:38.660
It has no effective handle whatsoever.

45:38.800 --> 45:39.660
You cannot feel it.

45:39.840 --> 45:43.740
And crucially, this recursive suspicion is intensely asymmetric, isn't it?

45:43.820 --> 45:45.240
It is deeply asymmetric.

45:45.540 --> 45:50.020
People will ruthlessly, obsessively scrutinize the funding, incentives,

45:50.020 --> 45:54.800
and historical errors of out-group institutions like the New York Times or the WHO.

45:55.460 --> 45:58.800
But they will completely ignore the massive financial incentives,

45:59.340 --> 46:00.740
algorithmic selection effects,

46:00.740 --> 46:06.180
and obvious biases of the podcasters, influencers, and anonymous substack writers

46:06.180 --> 46:09.620
operating safely inside their own trusted enclave.

46:09.740 --> 46:12.360
The provenance check only applies in one direction.

46:12.360 --> 46:17.080
Which is absolutely terrifying, because it means our society literally lacks the shared

46:17.080 --> 46:21.120
infrastructure to communicate complex truths across tribal lines.

46:21.300 --> 46:22.120
We've lost the grid.

46:22.360 --> 46:26.680
If shouting statistics doesn't work, and if trust is permanently locked inside these

46:26.680 --> 46:29.320
high-commitment enclaves, how do we fix the grid?

46:29.440 --> 46:30.380
How do we build a bridge?

46:31.000 --> 46:32.540
This brings us to the critical question.

46:32.980 --> 46:35.960
How do we actually establish accessible trustworthiness?

46:36.280 --> 46:41.640
The first step is acknowledging that the solution is not to demand that the public blindly defer to experts.

46:41.640 --> 46:45.160
You cannot just command a fragmented society to trust you.

46:45.600 --> 46:49.340
That structural dependence on expertise has to be earned back, piece by piece.

46:49.480 --> 46:51.520
So no more just saying, trust the science.

46:51.780 --> 46:52.760
No, that's done.

46:53.080 --> 46:56.880
The philosopher Onora O'Neill draws a vital, profound distinction here.

46:57.680 --> 47:03.520
Institutions should focus their energy on demonstrating trustworthiness, rather than demanding trust.

47:03.680 --> 47:04.600
I love that distinction.

47:05.020 --> 47:07.020
Trust is an emotion you cannot control.

47:07.520 --> 47:09.520
Trustworthiness is a behavior you can exhibit.

47:09.640 --> 47:10.080
Exactly.

47:10.080 --> 47:12.580
But what does that actually look like in practice?

47:13.340 --> 47:17.860
How does a massive, faceless institution act trustworthy on a Tuesday afternoon?

47:18.480 --> 47:22.340
It means institutions must become assessibly worthy of reliance.

47:22.880 --> 47:28.040
They have to radically change their reporting and communication practices to empower the individual.

47:28.240 --> 47:29.740
Give me some concrete examples.

47:29.880 --> 47:33.040
For example, they need to restore denominators next to numerators.

47:33.040 --> 47:40.820
Stop reporting raw injury counts without explicitly providing the baseline context of how often those events happen naturally.

47:40.820 --> 47:41.260
Right.

47:41.440 --> 47:43.060
The base rate problem we talked about.

47:43.200 --> 47:43.500
Exactly.

47:43.940 --> 47:47.960
They need to explicitly, repeatedly state the causal standards they are using.

47:48.140 --> 47:53.180
Are we talking about a VAIRS tripwire or are we talking about an adjudicated scientific link?

47:53.380 --> 47:55.820
Stop smashing the stages of safety together.

47:55.820 --> 47:56.420
Yes.

47:56.920 --> 48:04.720
And when they make a mistake, and they will, they need to make their corrections highly visible rather than quietly editing a web page.

48:05.040 --> 48:12.160
They have to structurally separate the raw underlying evidence from the reputation of the institution presenting it.

48:12.160 --> 48:20.920
You have to give the public the raw tools to check the reasoning rather than asking them to accept the final conclusion purely on the faith of the institution's historical prestige.

48:21.220 --> 48:22.000
You hit the nail on the head.

48:22.000 --> 48:24.700
Okay, let me challenge the practicality of this for a second.

48:25.680 --> 48:27.940
Realistically, I am a busy person.

48:28.160 --> 48:29.100
I have a job.

48:29.300 --> 48:29.980
I have a family.

48:30.160 --> 48:33.740
I cannot audit a multi-country mortality model on my lunch break.

48:33.980 --> 48:37.780
I don't have the statistical chops to calculate freshet inequalities.

48:38.080 --> 48:38.880
Most people don't.

48:38.880 --> 48:45.700
Isn't some level of absolute blind trust just a structural requirement of living in a modern, hyper-specialized society?

48:46.200 --> 48:49.200
The philosopher John Hardwig calls this epistemic dependence.

48:50.340 --> 48:55.720
We have to rely on experts because human knowledge is simply too vast for one brain to hold.

48:56.080 --> 48:58.640
I don't know how my car engine works, but I trust the mechanic.

48:58.640 --> 49:00.380
You are entirely correct.

49:00.600 --> 49:05.900
And acknowledging our inevitable epistemic dependence is a reality of modern life.

49:06.020 --> 49:06.140
Right.

49:06.140 --> 49:09.240
We cannot all be virologists, and we cannot all be mechanics.

49:09.480 --> 49:09.680
Right.

49:09.880 --> 49:18.100
But the goal of building accessible trustworthiness isn't to force universal, independent auditing by every single citizen.

49:18.340 --> 49:19.340
That's impossible.

49:19.580 --> 49:20.400
So what is the goal?

49:20.660 --> 49:28.400
The goal is creating an environmental culture where the capacity for independent verification is highly visible and culturally felt.

49:28.400 --> 49:43.860
Even if you personally never run the math on the global mortality model, the mere fact that the data is open source, the methodology is radically transparent, and the denominator, as clearly stated, diffuses the ambient paranoia of recursive suspicion.

49:44.360 --> 49:49.500
It signals to your brain that the institution is actively willing to be checked by anyone who has the time.

49:49.600 --> 49:50.060
Exactly.

49:50.360 --> 49:57.240
That willingness lowers the emotional temperature, diffuses the defensive posture, and slowly allows epistemic transport to resume.

49:57.240 --> 49:59.000
It's about showing the work.

49:59.660 --> 50:01.780
It's like being back in high school math class.

50:02.540 --> 50:10.100
If the teacher makes you show your work on the chalkboard step by step, you trust the final answer more than if they just pull a number out of a hat.

50:10.500 --> 50:16.880
Even if you can't fully follow all the advanced calculus they used in the middle, the transparency itself is the signal.

50:16.880 --> 50:17.900
Precisely.

50:17.900 --> 50:23.100
The phenomenon we are facing as a society isn't that human beings have suddenly stopped trusting altogether.

50:23.560 --> 50:36.460
It's that the specific claims requiring the most trust to transport, like invisible, counterfactual, population-level benefits, are the ones least able to survive the journey in our current fragmented environment.

50:36.460 --> 50:41.140
We have to purposefully rebuild the pathways for those specific, fragile claims.

50:41.340 --> 50:42.000
We really do.

50:42.420 --> 50:45.040
Well, let's recap the incredible journey we've been on today.

50:45.400 --> 50:49.080
We started by looking at what seemed like a simple, almost silly math error.

50:49.580 --> 50:55.660
The false partition, the assumption that the thousand lives saved and the one life injured are completely different people.

50:55.800 --> 50:57.540
The foundation of so much misunderstanding.

50:57.540 --> 51:05.400
We learned about the fundamental problem of causal inference and how the counterfactual, the death you didn't die, is permanently invisible.

51:06.000 --> 51:17.900
We explored how the media constantly mashes together highly modeled global estimates with raw, unverified VA ears reports, creating massive category errors that confuse the public.

51:18.000 --> 51:19.180
The mismatched numerators.

51:19.180 --> 51:31.320
We talked about the asymmetry of visibility, where present harm creates loud, motivated witnesses, but absent benefit leaves no trace at all, a phenomenon that is physically supercharged by the nocebo effect.

51:31.600 --> 51:32.420
Which was huge.

51:32.740 --> 51:36.080
And then we zoomed out to see how all of this feeds into the trust apocalypse.

51:36.760 --> 51:47.560
Not a single terrifying event, but a slow, predictable, sociological process of decline, fragmentation, and migration toward high-commitment enclaves where costly signals rule.

51:47.560 --> 51:49.800
People searching for CREDs.

51:49.800 --> 51:54.480
Finally, we learned that because of recursive suspicion, we have lost epistemic transport.

51:55.060 --> 52:06.400
The facts simply can't cross the tribal borders anymore, and the only way to fix it is through the slow, grueling work of accessible trustworthiness, not just demanding blind obedience from a skeptical public.

52:06.560 --> 52:11.020
It is a profound, albeit difficult, diagnosis of our current epistemic crisis.

52:11.020 --> 52:26.520
But I think it's vital because it moves the conversation past simple, lazy accusations of misinformation and forces us to look at the structural, psychological mechanics of how we actually process and communicate risk as a species.

52:26.520 --> 52:30.840
And that brings me to our final provocative thought for this deep dive.

52:31.080 --> 52:35.400
I want you, the listener, to really mull this over as you go about your day.

52:35.860 --> 52:46.200
We spend billions of dollars, endless hours, and the brightest minds of our generation researching new medical technologies, new public policies, and new interventions to save lives.

52:46.320 --> 52:47.740
We put everything we have into it.

52:47.740 --> 52:57.920
But if an intervention's success intrinsically removes the very evidence needed to convince people that it works, maybe the next great leap in human progress won't happen in a laboratory at all.

52:58.100 --> 52:58.740
Oh, that's interesting.

52:58.740 --> 53:02.080
Maybe it has to happen in the way we build the architecture of our own communication.

53:02.720 --> 53:07.840
If you were in charge of communicating an invisible miracle, how would you make the world see it?

